Data in engine/materials/profiles/ and engine/library/mouth/
-----------------------------------------------------------
Each value in a material profile names its source and that source's licence status (the "src", "cite" and "lic"
fields). Most values are physical quantities (densities, moduli, friction coefficients, reflectances) taken as facts
from the cited publications; no text, table or dataset is reproduced from a source whose licence does not allow it.
Sources marked public domain (United States government works) and CC0 (for example refractiveindex.info) need no
attribution and are cited in the profiles.

Every source is attributed in the profile that uses it and listed, with its licence class, in docs/DATA_SOURCES.md
(generated from the profiles' own source blocks).

  CC BY-SA 4.0 data files: engine/materials/profiles/light.sky.json, light.studio.json and light.sun.json are
  licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0 as whole files (not Apache-2.0), because each carries a 33-value excerpt of:
    CIE (2019) CIE standard illuminant D65. International Commission on Illumination, Vienna.
      doi:10.25039/CIE.DS.hjfjmt59. https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

  Facts only (no text, table or figure reproduced), named here because their terms limit reuse of the works
  themselves:
    Sources under non-commercial licences (CC BY-NC, CC BY-NC-SA, CC BY-NC-ND), for example Pakpour et al. (2012)
      Sci Rep 2:549 in sand.wet.json: findings stated in our own words as facts.
    The IT'IS Foundation tissue properties database: individual averages cited as facts in the tissue profiles;
      the database is not copied.
    An accepted manuscript on abdominal adipose tissue (Calvo-Gallego et al.) in fat.subcutaneous.json: fitted
      constants and points read from one published curve, used as facts.
    Gent AN (1958), Rubber Chem Technol 31(4):896-906, doi:10.5254/1.3542351: the hardness-to-modulus formula, in
      rubber.sole.json.

The neutral studio environment (engine/fieldstudio/fieldstudio/gpu/studio_env.py) is generated from a formula; no
image is used. The tool's glyphs and icons are drawn by its own code.

